# Edge hardening + merchant-profile routing — 2026-07-26
#
# APPLIED LIVE on the VPS 2026-07-26 (unlike the PREPARE-ONLY snippets beside it).
# Backups taken before the change:
#   /root/nginx-backups-20260726/tilla.gudman.xyz.conf.PRE-FIX
#   /root/nginx-backups-20260726/nginx.conf.PRE-FIX
# `nginx -t` was run before every reload; all sibling sites on the shared box
# (warden / verdikt / bequest / gapguard / reef / shop) re-verified 200 after.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. FIX: /m/{address} merchant profiles were 404 in public
#
# `sitemap.xml` emits one `/m/{address}` link per merchant with a public live
# store (app/main.py, the `merchant_profile` handler answers them), but the vhost
# had no `location /m/`, so every one of those URLs fell through to
# `location / { root /opt/tilla/www; }` and returned the static 404. The app was
# serving them correctly on 127.0.0.1:8040 the whole time — this was purely an
# edge-routing gap, and it meant the sitemap advertised four dead URLs to
# crawlers. Added immediately above the static `location /`:
#
#     location /m/ { proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8040; proxy_set_header Host $host; proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr; proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for; proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto https; }
#
# Prefix (not exact) match, because the address is part of the path. It sits
# above `location /` but cannot shadow `location /s/` — different prefix.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Security headers at server level
#
# The vhost previously sent only X-Content-Type-Options and Referrer-Policy, and
# sent them WITHOUT `always`, so they were dropped on error responses. There was
# no HSTS anywhere and no X-Frame-Options outside `location /s/` — meaning the
# dashboard, the page that drives wallet `personal_sign` sign-in and refund
# actions, was framable by any origin. Server block now sends:
#
#     add_header X-Content-Type-Options nosniff always;
#     add_header Referrer-Policy strict-origin-when-cross-origin always;
#     add_header X-Frame-Options SAMEORIGIN always;
#     add_header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=300" always;
#     add_header Permissions-Policy "geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=()" always;
#
# HSTS started at a deliberately short max-age=300 and was RAISED to 31536000
# (one year) later the same day, once renewal was actually proven rather than
# assumed. The evidence, since "the cert is valid until October" is not evidence:
#   * `snap.certbot.renew.timer` is active and had fired unattended that morning
#     (04:25 CEST) — but the cert was not due, so that run was a no-op.
#   * `certbot renew --cert-name tilla.gudman.xyz --dry-run` → "all simulated
#     renewals succeeded". Same for shop.gudman.xyz. That exercises the real
#     webroot challenge end-to-end, which the no-op timer run does not.
# Both renewals use `authenticator = webroot` with NO installer, so a dry run
# never touches nginx — safe to run against a live site.
# Deliberately NOT added: `preload` (a submission to a public browser list,
# effectively irreversible) and `includeSubDomains` (nothing is hosted under
# tilla.gudman.xyz, so it would buy nothing and would trap a future http
# sub-subdomain).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Header inheritance inside `location /s/`
#
# GOTCHA worth remembering: nginx `add_header` does NOT merge across levels — a
# single `add_header` inside a location cancels ALL server-level ones. Because
# `location /s/` declared its own two, store pages were silently missing
# Referrer-Policy. Every header is now restated inside that block. Any future
# location that adds a header must restate the full set too.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. gzip for JSON/JS (/etc/nginx/nginx.conf, not the vhost)
#
# `gzip on;` was set but `gzip_types` stayed commented out, and nginx's default
# is `text/html` ONLY — so every JSON feed, the discovery API, and embed.js
# shipped uncompressed. Uncommented and widened:
#
#     gzip_types text/plain text/css application/json application/javascript text/javascript application/xml text/xml image/svg+xml;
#
# Measured effect: GET /discovery/resources 16782 -> 2877 bytes on the wire.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. Removed 9 stale vhost copies from sites-enabled
#
# `nginx.conf` does `include /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/*` — an unfiltered glob, so
# it was loading 9 `tilla.gudman.xyz.conf.bak-*` / `.bak.preproduct` files as real
# server blocks. `nginx -t` printed a `conflicting server name` warning for each,
# and the live config won only because `.conf` sorts before `.bak-*`. A rename or
# a differently-sorting filename could silently have served `bak-m4`, which
# predates /dashboard, /api/merchant and the rate limits. All nine moved to
# /root/nginx-backups-20260726/; `nginx -t` is now free of conflicting-name
# warnings. Keep vhost backups OUTSIDE sites-enabled.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. limit_req on the write endpoints and /mcp — APPLIED
#
# The `tilla_create` zone (12r/m) was DEFINED in conf.d/tilla-limit-req.conf and
# referenced NOWHERE, so the three paid write endpoints ran unthrottled — and
# /create-store both spends an LLM call and writes a store to disk. /mcp was
# likewise open. Now:
#
#     location = /create-store   limit_req zone=tilla_create burst=5  nodelay;
#     location = /upgrade-store  limit_req zone=tilla_create burst=5  nodelay;
#     location = /add-product    limit_req zone=tilla_create burst=5  nodelay;
#     location = /mcp            limit_req zone=tilla_api    burst=20 nodelay;
#
# (/api/ and the checkout_sessions regex were already on tilla_api.)
# Backup before this step: tilla.gudman.xyz.conf.PRE-RATELIMIT.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. CSP — the half that needs nothing threaded through — APPLIED
#
#     add_header Content-Security-Policy "frame-ancestors 'self'; base-uri 'self'; object-src 'none'; form-action 'self'" always;
#
# `script-src`/`style-src` are NOT here and are NOT a same-day change: the pages
# inline all their CSS and JS, so restricting those needs a nonce or hash threaded
# through the store generator and every static page. These four directives need
# none of that and cannot break inline code. Verified safe before applying —
# grepped www/ and themes/ for `<form>`, `<base>`, `<object>/<embed>/<applet>`:
# the site uses NONE of them, so nothing can regress. What they buy:
#   frame-ancestors  clickjacking cover that also applies where XFO is ignored
#   base-uri         a `<base>` injection can't repoint every relative URL
#   object-src       no plugin content, ever
#   form-action      injected markup can't POST a form to another origin
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 8. shop.gudman.xyz got the same treatment
#
# The custom-domain vhost (/etc/nginx/sites-available/shop.gudman.xyz.conf, which
# sites-enabled symlinks) previously sent only nosniff + Referrer-Policy, without
# `always`. It now carries the identical six headers. Its cert renewal was
# dry-run-proven first, same as tilla's. Backup: shop.gudman.xyz.conf.PRE-HEADERS.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9. Cache-Control on the nginx-served statics — APPLIED
#
# App-served routes already set their own (embed.js 3600, /discovery 300); only
# files nginx reads off disk had ETag/Last-Modified alone.
#
#     location ~* ^/[^/]+\.(png|svg|ico|webp|jpg|jpeg|gif|woff2)$ { expires 1h; }
#     location /   { add_header Cache-Control "no-cache" always; }
#
# HTML gets `no-cache`, NOT a max-age: these pages list live stores and prices, and
# a stale copy in an intermediary is worse than a revalidation round-trip.
# `no-cache` still permits a 304 against the existing ETag, so the cost is one
# conditional request, not a re-download. Do not add `expires` alongside an
# `add_header Cache-Control` — `expires` emits Cache-Control itself and you get
# the header twice (this happened; the duplicate was removed).
#
# ⚠️ THE REGEX MUST STAY ANCHORED TO A SINGLE PATH SEGMENT (`^/[^/]+\.`).
# It was first written as a bare `~* \.(png|svg|...)$`, which — because a regex
# location beats a prefix location — hijacked `/s/{slug}/og.png` away from
# `location /s/` and resolved it under `root /opt/tilla/www` instead of the store
# alias. Result: EVERY store's og.png and og.svg 404'd, i.e. every store page lost
# its social card, while the platform pages looked fine. Caught only by explicitly
# re-testing store assets after the change. If you widen this regex, re-test
# `/s/<slug>/og.png` before you walk away.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 10. limit_req on the last two open groups — APPLIED
#
#     location /feeds/                  limit_req zone=tilla_api burst=30 nodelay;
#     location ~ ^/s/…/(buy|mcp|…)$     limit_req zone=tilla_api burst=40 nodelay;
#
# Bursts are deliberately generous: the store-action regex covers `buy`, so a
# limit tight enough to bite would block a real purchase or a judge trying one.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 11. /favicon.ico + /favicon.svg — APPLIED
#
# The pages carry the icon inline as a data: URI, so tabs always rendered, but
# `/favicon.ico` itself was a 404 — which is what crawlers, bookmark importers and
# feed readers request blind. The mark is now a real file (`www/favicon.svg`) plus
# a genuine ICO built by `scripts/make_favicon.py`. The box has rsvg-convert but no
# ImageMagick / icotool / png2ico, so that script assembles the ICO container by
# hand around a 32x32 PNG (PNG-in-ICO is a valid .ico, read by every current
# browser and by the Windows shell — not a PNG with the extension changed).
# `file` confirms: "MS Windows icon resource - 1 icon, 32x32 with PNG image data".
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT DONE — the one item genuinely left
#
# * CSP `script-src` / `style-src`. Not deferred out of caution — deferred because
#   it is a real change: every page inlines its CSS and JS (the landing page alone
#   has 3 <script> and 2 <style> blocks), and the store pages are generated from
#   themes. Hashes would have to be recomputed on every copy edit; nonces need a
#   per-response template pass, which nginx-served static files do not get. Doing
#   it properly means moving the static pages behind the app or building the CSP
#   in the generator. See item 7 for the half that needed none of that.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT A DEFECT, checked and recorded so nobody re-opens it
#
# * `https://<custom-domain>/s/{slug}/` is a 404, and that is correct. Store
#   HTML is served by nginx from /opt/tilla/stores/ on the PLATFORM host only;
#   the app has no /s/{slug}/ HTML route. A custom domain serves its store at
#   its ROOT (`custom_domain_root`), which is what the canonical URL points at.
#   Only the app-routed children (/s/{slug}/feed.json etc.) answer under /s/
#   there, because those live on the agentic router.
